; ======================================================================
; GOODIX GT9xx CAPACITIVE TOUCH CONTROLLER for the Raspberry Pi 4 -
; library, not a compiler builtin. Up to TEN simultaneous touches, I2C.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Written for the touch half of the Waveshare 10.1" MIPI DSI panel, which
; carries a Goodix part on the SAME I2C pair the display connector brings
; out. It is not panel-specific: any GT9xx that answers at 0x5D or 0x14
; and speaks the register map below will work.
;
; USE - all three includes, in this order, in the MAIN file:
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"       ; i2c.pi4 needs it
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4"        ; REQUIRED, before this
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/touch_goodix.pi4"
;
; Libraries do not include libraries (project ruling, 2026-07-30), so this
; file names no other .pi4. If i2c.pi4 is missing above it, the build
; fails LOUDLY on an undefined I2cWriteRead, which is the designed failure.
;
;     I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_DISPLAY) : I2cUp()
;     If Gt9Begin() <> 0
;       If Gt9Poll() > 0
;         rx = Gt9RawX(0) : ry = Gt9RawY(0)     ; the CONTROLLER's units
;       EndIf
;     EndIf
;
; ======================================================================
; THIS FILE REPORTS THE CONTROLLER'S OWN COORDINATES AND NOTHING ELSE
; ======================================================================
; There is no screen transform in here, and its absence is the design.
;
; A touch point becomes a point on a SCREEN by going through the display
; layer's rotation and its scale - the same rotation and the same scale
; the console's own pixels go through, in ONE place
; (RaspberryPi4/Board/screen_geom.pi4, ScrTouchX / ScrTouchY). A second
; copy of that arithmetic living in the touch driver would be a second
; opinion about which way up the panel is, and the two would disagree the
; first time somebody typed `screen rotate`.
;
; So: this file answers "where on the CONTROLLER", in the controller's
; own units, with Gt9XMax() and Gt9YMax() saying how big those units are.
; The board maps it. A driver that knew which connector it was on, or
; which way up the frame was bolted to a bench, would be the wrong shape.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE CONTROLLER'S RESOLUTION IS READ, NOT ASSUMED
; ======================================================================
; goodix.c:1072 goodix_read_config reads the config block and takes the
; touch resolution straight out of it:
;
;     x_max = le16(config[RESOLUTION_LOC])      RESOLUTION_LOC   = 1
;     y_max = le16(config[RESOLUTION_LOC + 2])
;     max_touch_num = min(config[MAX_CONTACTS_LOC] & 0x0f, 10)
;                                               MAX_CONTACTS_LOC = 5
;     int_trigger_type = config[TRIGGER_LOC] & 0x03
;                                               TRIGGER_LOC      = 6
;
; and byte 0 of the same block is the config VERSION. Seven bytes at
; 0x8047 carry all five numbers, so Gt9Begin reads seven.
;
; THAT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. The device tree for the panel on this
; bench (10_1_inch_a) sets NO touchscreen-size-x, NO touchscreen-size-y
; and no axis override at all - see the table below - so the controller's
; own config block is the ONLY statement of its resolution anywhere. A
; driver that hardcoded 800 by 1280 would be right about this panel by
; luck and silently wrong about the next one, and being wrong about it
; does not look like an error: it looks like a finger that lands a
; hand's width from where it touched.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THE OVERLAY SAYS ABOUT THE PANEL ON THIS BENCH, LINE BY LINE
; ======================================================================
; RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_vc4-kms-dsi-waveshare-panel-v2-overlay.dts
;
;     touch: goodix@5d {
;         compatible = "goodix,gt9271";
;         reg = <0x5d>;
;         reset-gpio = <&display_mcu 9 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
;     };
;
; Three things follow and every one of them shapes this file:
;
;   1. THE ADDRESS IS 0x5D AND THE BOARD DECIDED IT. A GT9xx picks 0x5D
;      or 0x14 from the level on its INT pin while RESET is released.
;      goodix.c:783 goodix_reset_no_int_sync is the code that drives
;      that dance - and it runs only when the driver has BOTH an INT and
;      a RESET line (goodix.c:1055). This node declares a reset and NO
;      interrupt, so goodix_get_gpio_config leaves irq_pin_access_method
;      at IRQ_PIN_ACCESS_NONE (goodix.c:1047) and probe never calls
;      goodix_reset at all (goodix.c:1396, guarded by
;      reset_controller_at_probe, which only that branch sets).
;
;      SO THERE IS NO HANDSHAKE TO PERFORM HERE, and that is not a gap
;      in this driver: the reference driver does not perform one on this
;      wiring either. The address is a fact about the board. This file
;      PROBES for it - 0x5D first, then 0x14 - and never tries to move
;      one.
;
;   2. THE RESET LINE IS NOT ON ANY Pi GPIO. It is bit 9 of the GPIO
;      expander inside the panel's own micro at 0x45, ten addresses away
;      on the same bus. THE GOODIX CANNOT ANSWER UNTIL THAT MICRO HAS
;      BEEN WRITTEN: the regulator's probe writes the shadow 0x0300 -
;      VCC on, touch reset released - and waits 20 ms before anything
;      else happens on the bus (waveshare-panel-regulator.c:232-236).
;      A touch scan that runs first reads as a dead bus on a perfectly
;      good panel. That write is the caller's job; Gt9Begin() cannot do
;      it, because which micro sits on which bus is board knowledge.
;
;   3. THERE IS NO INTERRUPT, SO POLL ON A CLOCK. Neither Waveshare
;      overlay gives the Goodix node an `interrupts` or an
;      `interrupt-parent` property. Linux handles exactly this wiring
;      with a polled timer at GOODIX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS = 17, commented
;      "17ms = 60fps" (goodix.c:51). So Gt9Poll() belongs on a tick at
;      about 60 Hz, not in a tight loop: each call is up to three I2C
;      transactions and every one can spend its full spin guard when the
;      bus does not answer. A finger is slower than 60 Hz and always
;      will be.
;
; AND THE AXES ARE NOT OVERRIDDEN FOR THIS PANEL. The v2 overlay's
; __overrides__ list gives touchscreen-inverted-x to 9_0_inch_b,
; touchscreen-inverted-y to 10_1_inch_b, and swapped-x-y + inverted-y to
; 12_3_inch_a_4lane - and gives 10_1_inch_a, the panel on this bench,
; NOTHING. So the controller's axes are the panel's scan axes: touch x
; runs with the panel's 800 across and touch y with its 1280 down. The
; board's mapping starts from that and the board carries the per-panel
; correction (`touch axes`, stored) for a panel where it is not true -
; which is the same structure the overlay itself uses, one line per
; panel, and not a knob invented here.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE REPEATED START IS NOT OPTIONAL
; ======================================================================
; The register pointer is SIXTEEN BITS and it is written as two bytes,
; HIGH BYTE FIRST. The controller then expects the read to follow WITHOUT
; A STOP - a repeated start (goodix.c:171, two i2c_msg, the second
; I2C_M_RD, one i2c_transfer).
;
; Put a STOP in between and the pointer is forgotten. The read still
; succeeds. It still returns bytes. They are simply from wherever the
; pointer happened to be, which after a previous read is somewhere
; plausible in the same register map. That is the silent-wrong-answer
; shape in its purest form: a driver that appears to work, tracks
; nothing, and blames the panel.
;
; i2c.pi4's two-transaction I2cWrite-then-I2cRead path therefore CANNOT
; be used here, and this file calls I2cWriteRead() instead. That
; procedure did not exist until this driver needed it; its comment block
; carries the recipe out of BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 3.3.2.
;
; The WRITE direction has no such problem - pointer and data go out in
; one write transaction with a normal STOP at the end - so Gt9WriteReg
; uses plain I2cWrite.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PRODUCT ID IS THE PROBE, NOT AN ADDRESS ACK
; ======================================================================
; An address ACK says "something is here". The four ASCII bytes at
; 0x8140 say what: a GT911 answers "911" and a GT9271 answers "9271"
; (goodix.c:98-118 lists every id the reference driver knows). On a bus
; that also carries a panel micro, the weaker answer is the one that
; misleads, so Gt9TryAddr reads the identity and keeps it.
;
; WHICH ADDRESS ANSWERED ALSO SUGGESTS THE PANEL GENERATION, and on a
; first bring-up that is worth more than the touch itself:
;
;     0x5D  -> GT9271 -> the "DSI-TOUCH" v2 family, 800x1280 portrait,
;                        a panel that needs a 200-command DCS table
;     0x14  -> GT911  -> the v1 family, 1280x800 landscape, a panel that
;                        needs no DSI init sequence at all
;
; It is suggestive and not conclusive - the address is chosen by a pin
; and the same part number ships both ways - which is exactly why the id
; string is read rather than the address believed.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE BUFFER-READY FLAG MUST BE CLEARED, OR THE SAME TOUCH REPEATS FOREVER
; ======================================================================
; Register 0x814E's high bit says "a frame is ready" and the low nibble is
; the touch count. The controller produces no further frame until that
; register is written back as 0 (goodix.c:515, :529).
;
; Gt9Poll() clears it on every path THAT SAW A FRAME - including the frame
; that reported zero touches, and including the one where the coordinate
; read then failed.
;
; It deliberately does NOT clear when the high bit was low. That is not a
; missed path: a low high bit means the controller is still assembling a
; frame, and writing 0 over that throws away the frame being built.
;
; AND THE FLAG IS LATE. goodix.c: "The 'buffer status' bit ... is not set
; as soon as the interrupt is raised, but slightly after. This takes
; around 10 ms to happen, so we poll for 20 ms." That is why a single
; read that finds the bit clear means "not yet", never "no touch", and
; why #GT9_ENOTREADY is the commonest return this file has.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHERE THE REGISTER MAP COMES FROM (do not guess - rule 21)
; ======================================================================
; RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_goodix.h and _goodix.c, fetched
; 2026-09-06 from
; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raspberrypi/linux/rpi-6.12.y/
; drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.{c,h}
;
;   goodix.h:34  GOODIX_REG_COMMAND         0x8040
;   goodix.h:38  GOODIX_REG_SW_WDT          0x8041
;   goodix.h:48  GOODIX_REG_STATUS          0x8044
;   goodix.h:51  GOODIX_GT9X_REG_CONFIG_DATA 0x8047
;   goodix.h:52  GOODIX_REG_ID              0x8140
;   goodix.h:53  GOODIX_READ_COOR_ADDR      0x814E
;   goodix.h:56  GOODIX_ID_MAX_LEN          4
;   goodix.c:34  GOODIX_CONTACT_SIZE        8
;   goodix.c:36  GOODIX_MAX_CONTACTS        10
;   goodix.c:43  GOODIX_BUFFER_STATUS_READY BIT(7)
;   goodix.c:45  GOODIX_BUFFER_STATUS_TIMEOUT 20   (milliseconds)
;   goodix.c:47  RESOLUTION_LOC 1, MAX_CONTACTS_LOC 5, TRIGGER_LOC 6
;   goodix.c:51  GOODIX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS    17
;   goodix.c:171 goodix_i2c_read  - two-byte big-endian pointer, then a
;                                   repeated-start read
;   goodix.c:412 goodix_ts_report_touch_8b - id in bits 3..0 of byte 0,
;                                   X at [1..2] LE, Y at [3..4] LE,
;                                   width at [5..6] LE
;   goodix.c:515 the buffer release: write 0 to GOODIX_READ_COOR_ADDR
;   goodix.c:1111 goodix_read_version - four id bytes then a 16-bit LE
;                                   firmware version at [4..5]
;
; The 9-byte contact variant in goodix.c exists only behind a DMI quirk
; list for x86 tablets (goodix.c:1236). This part is not one, and a
; contact size that is a setting nobody can check is worse than a
; constant that is cited, so 8 is a constant here.
;
; House prior art on two other targets, same chip family, and worth
; reading before changing anything here: RP2350/Lib/gt911.pico2 and
; RP2040/Lib/gt911.pico. They cap at five points because their panel
; does; this one goes to ten because the controller does and the
; Waveshare panel is sold on it.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The two addresses, and what each one suggests about the panel.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GT9_ADDR_HIGH  = $5D         ; GT9271 - Waveshare v2, 800x1280 portrait
#GT9_ADDR_LOW   = $14         ; GT911  - Waveshare v1, 1280x800 landscape

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Registers. Sixteen bits, written high byte first.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GT9_REG_COMMAND = $8040
#GT9_REG_STATUS  = $8044
#GT9_REG_CONFIG  = $8047      ; the config block - see the header
#GT9_REG_ID      = $8140      ; 4 ASCII id bytes, then 2 bytes of version
#GT9_REG_COOR    = $814E      ; the buffer-status byte, then the contacts

#GT9_CMD_SCREEN_OFF = $05     ; goodix.h:35, written to #GT9_REG_COMMAND

#GT9_STATUS_READY = $80       ; bit 7 of the byte at #GT9_REG_COOR
#GT9_COUNT_MASK   = $0F       ; bits 3..0 of the same byte

#GT9_CONTACT_SIZE = 8
#GT9_MAX_POINTS   = 10
#GT9_ID_LEN       = 4

; The config block's first seven bytes carry every number this driver
; wants out of it. goodix.c reads 186 or 240 depending on the part
; because it also CHECKSUMS and REWRITES the block; nothing here writes
; a config, so nothing here needs the rest of it.
#GT9_CFG_HEAD     = 7
#GT9_CFG_RES      = 1         ; RESOLUTION_LOC   - x_max, then y_max, LE16
#GT9_CFG_CONTACTS = 5         ; MAX_CONTACTS_LOC - low nibble
#GT9_CFG_TRIGGER  = 6         ; TRIGGER_LOC      - low two bits

; The largest single read this driver ever does: the one-byte header plus
; ten contacts plus the trailing key byte. Sized once, here, so no call
; site has to get the arithmetic right.
#GT9_BUF_BYTES = 1 + (#GT9_CONTACT_SIZE * #GT9_MAX_POINTS) + 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Result codes. Gt9Begin returns an ADDRESS or 0; these are for the
;  procedures that report a reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GT9_OK        = 0
#GT9_ENODEV    = -1           ; Gt9Begin has not found a controller
#GT9_EBUS      = -2           ; the transfer failed - see Gt9LastBusCode()
#GT9_ENOTREADY = -3           ; no frame was ready this poll (not an error)
#GT9_EPROTO    = -4           ; the controller reported more touches than
                              ; its own config block has slots for

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  State.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gt9_addr.i             ; 0 = nothing found yet
Global gt9_count.i            ; touches in the last frame that HAD one
Global gt9_fw.i               ; 16-bit firmware version from 0x8140+4
Global gt9_cfg.i              ; config version byte from 0x8047, -1 unread
Global gt9_xmax.i             ; the controller's own x range, 0 if unread
Global gt9_ymax.i             ; and its y range
Global gt9_maxpts.i           ; contact slots the config block declares
Global gt9_trigger.i          ; the trigger type it declares, -1 if unread
Global gt9_buscode.i          ; the last #I2C_* code any transfer returned
Global gt9_codehigh.i         ; what $5D said when Gt9Begin last asked
Global gt9_codelow.i          ; what $14 said, or #I2C_OK if never asked
Global gt9_frames.i           ; frames seen since Gt9Begin
Global gt9_badframes.i        ; frames refused for an impossible count

Global Dim gt9_buf.a[#GT9_BUF_BYTES]
Global Dim gt9_idstr.a[#GT9_ID_LEN + 1]
Global Dim gt9_id.i[#GT9_MAX_POINTS]
Global Dim gt9_x.i[#GT9_MAX_POINTS]
Global Dim gt9_y.i[#GT9_MAX_POINTS]
Global Dim gt9_sz.i[#GT9_MAX_POINTS]

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9ReadReg(reg, n) - read n bytes from a 16-bit register into gt9_buf.
;
;  ONE transaction with a repeated start. See the header for why a STOP
;  here would return believable rubbish instead of failing.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with the bus code left in gt9_buscode.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9ReadReg(reg.i, n.i)
  Define hdr0.a
  Define hdr1.a
  Define r.i
  Dim hdr.a[2]

  If gt9_addr = 0 Or n < 1 Or n > #GT9_BUF_BYTES
    gt9_buscode = #I2C_ARG
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; HIGH byte first. The other order reads a completely different part of
  ; the map and returns numbers that look like data.
  hdr0 = (reg >> 8) & $FF
  hdr1 = reg & $FF
  hdr[0] = hdr0
  hdr[1] = hdr1

  r = I2cWriteRead(gt9_addr, @hdr, 2, @gt9_buf, n)
  gt9_buscode = r
  If r <> #I2C_OK
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9WriteReg(reg, v) - write one byte to a 16-bit register.
;
;  A plain write transaction: pointer and data go out together and the
;  STOP at the end is correct. No repeated start is involved.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9WriteReg(reg.i, v.i)
  Define r.i
  Dim pkt.a[3]

  If gt9_addr = 0
    gt9_buscode = #I2C_ARG
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  pkt[0] = (reg >> 8) & $FF
  pkt[1] = reg & $FF
  pkt[2] = v & $FF

  r = I2cWrite(gt9_addr, @pkt, 3)
  gt9_buscode = r
  If r <> #I2C_OK
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9TryAddr(a) - is there a controller at this address? Reads the six
;  identity bytes and keeps them if it worked.
;
;  A PRODUCT ID READ IS THE PROBE, not an address ACK. See the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9TryAddr(a.i)
  Define i.i

  gt9_addr = a
  If Gt9ReadReg(#GT9_REG_ID, 6) = 0
    gt9_addr = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  i = 0
  While i < #GT9_ID_LEN
    gt9_idstr[i] = gt9_buf[i] & $FF
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  gt9_idstr[#GT9_ID_LEN] = 0

  ; goodix.c:1125 - get_unaligned_le16(&buf[4]).
  gt9_fw = (gt9_buf[4] & $FF) | ((gt9_buf[5] & $FF) << 8)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9ReadConfig() - the config block's first seven bytes: the version,
;  the touch resolution, the contact count and the trigger type.
;
;  FAILURE HERE IS NOT FATAL and that is deliberate. The controller is
;  present and answering identity; a driver that refused to start
;  because seven more bytes would not read has turned a diagnostic into
;  an outage. What it costs is the resolution, and the accessors say so
;  by answering 0 rather than a plausible number - see Gt9XMax().
;
;  Returns 1 if the block read, else 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9ReadConfig()
  Define n.i

  gt9_cfg = -1
  gt9_xmax = 0
  gt9_ymax = 0
  gt9_maxpts = #GT9_MAX_POINTS
  gt9_trigger = -1

  If Gt9ReadReg(#GT9_REG_CONFIG, #GT9_CFG_HEAD) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  gt9_cfg = gt9_buf[0] & $FF

  ; LITTLE-endian, both of them, exactly like the coordinates and unlike
  ; the register ADDRESS that fetched them.
  gt9_xmax = (gt9_buf[#GT9_CFG_RES] & $FF) | ((gt9_buf[#GT9_CFG_RES + 1] & $FF) << 8)
  gt9_ymax = (gt9_buf[#GT9_CFG_RES + 2] & $FF) | ((gt9_buf[#GT9_CFG_RES + 3] & $FF) << 8)

  ; goodix.c:1093 - min(config[5] & 0x0f, GOODIX_MAX_CONTACTS). A zero
  ; there is a config block that has not been programmed, and zero slots
  ; would refuse every frame the controller ever sends, so it falls back
  ; to the part's maximum the same way goodix.c:1229 does.
  n = gt9_buf[#GT9_CFG_CONTACTS] & $0F
  If n < 1 Or n > #GT9_MAX_POINTS
    n = #GT9_MAX_POINTS
  EndIf
  gt9_maxpts = n

  gt9_trigger = gt9_buf[#GT9_CFG_TRIGGER] & $03

  ; A resolution of zero in either axis is what an unprogrammed block
  ; reads as, and goodix.c:1213 treats exactly that as "invalid config,
  ; using defaults". Nothing here invents a number in its place: the
  ; caller is told 0 and can say so, because a made-up resolution puts
  ; every finger in the wrong place with no error anywhere.
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9Begin() - find the controller, learn what it is, and clear whatever
;  frame was left over from before we arrived.
;
;  Returns the 7-bit address found, or 0 for "no Goodix on this bus".
;
;  0x5D IS TRIED FIRST because that is the address the panel on this
;  bench answers on - the v2 overlay's goodix@5d node - and trying the
;  other one costs one failed transfer and means a v1 board is
;  IDENTIFIED rather than reported missing.
;
;  THE CALLER BRINGS THE BUS UP, AND ON A v2 PANEL THE CALLER MUST ALSO
;  HAVE WRITTEN THE PANEL MICRO FIRST. The touch controller's reset is
;  expander bit 9 inside that micro and nothing else on the board
;  reaches it; see point 2 of the header. Which bus the panel is on, and
;  whether its micro has been powered, are the board's questions and not
;  this driver's.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9Begin()
  gt9_addr = 0
  gt9_count = 0
  gt9_fw = 0
  gt9_cfg = -1
  gt9_xmax = 0
  gt9_ymax = 0
  gt9_maxpts = #GT9_MAX_POINTS
  gt9_trigger = -1
  gt9_frames = 0
  gt9_badframes = 0
  gt9_buscode = #I2C_OK
  gt9_codehigh = #I2C_OK
  gt9_codelow = #I2C_OK
  gt9_idstr[0] = 0

  ; BOTH CODES ARE KEPT, not just the last one. When nothing answers, a
  ; single "last bus code" is always the SECOND address's, and the
  ; second is the one we did not expect - so the interesting failure is
  ; the one that gets overwritten. A NACK at $5D means no chip there;
  ; anything else is a defect in the transfer and not a missing panel.
  If Gt9TryAddr(#GT9_ADDR_HIGH) = 0
    gt9_codehigh = gt9_buscode
    If Gt9TryAddr(#GT9_ADDR_LOW) = 0
      gt9_codelow = gt9_buscode
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  Gt9ReadConfig()

  ; Whatever frame was pending before we arrived is not ours.
  Gt9WriteReg(#GT9_REG_COOR, 0)
  ProcedureReturn gt9_addr
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9Poll() - read one frame if one is ready.
;
;  Returns the number of touches now down (0 to 10), or a negative
;  #GT9_E* code. ZERO IS NORMAL and means "a frame arrived and nothing is
;  touching the panel". #GT9_ENOTREADY means "no frame yet", which is also
;  normal and is what most calls return - the buffer-ready bit lags the
;  touch by about ten milliseconds.
;
;  Call this from a tick at about 60 Hz. See the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9Poll()
  Define st.i
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define base.i

  gt9_count = 0
  If gt9_addr = 0
    ProcedureReturn #GT9_ENODEV
  EndIf

  If Gt9ReadReg(#GT9_REG_COOR, 1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #GT9_EBUS
  EndIf
  st = gt9_buf[0] & $FF

  ; High bit clear: the controller is still assembling. NOTHING TO READ
  ; AND NOTHING TO CLEAR - writing 0 here would discard the frame being
  ; built. This is the common return.
  If (st & #GT9_STATUS_READY) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #GT9_ENOTREADY
  EndIf

  gt9_frames = gt9_frames + 1
  n = st & #GT9_COUNT_MASK

  If n > gt9_maxpts
    ; More contacts than the controller's own config block says it has
    ; slots for is not that many fingers, it is a corrupt read
    ; (goodix.c:280 returns -EPROTO on exactly this). Clear the flag so
    ; the controller is not left wedged on it, count it, and say so -
    ; rather than indexing off the end of four arrays.
    gt9_badframes = gt9_badframes + 1
    Gt9WriteReg(#GT9_REG_COOR, 0)
    ProcedureReturn #GT9_EPROTO
  EndIf

  If n > 0
    If Gt9ReadReg(#GT9_REG_COOR + 1, n * #GT9_CONTACT_SIZE) = 0
      ; The frame was real and we lost it. Still clear, or no further
      ; frame is ever produced.
      Gt9WriteReg(#GT9_REG_COOR, 0)
      ProcedureReturn #GT9_EBUS
    EndIf
    i = 0
    While i < n
      base = i * #GT9_CONTACT_SIZE
      ; LITTLE-endian here, unlike the register ADDRESS above which is
      ; BIG-endian. Getting those two the same way round is the classic
      ; GT9xx mistake: the coordinates then track the finger and land in
      ; the wrong place, which reads as a calibration problem for a week.
      gt9_id[i] = gt9_buf[base] & $0F
      gt9_x[i] = (gt9_buf[base + 1] & $FF) | ((gt9_buf[base + 2] & $FF) << 8)
      gt9_y[i] = (gt9_buf[base + 3] & $FF) | ((gt9_buf[base + 4] & $FF) << 8)
      gt9_sz[i] = (gt9_buf[base + 5] & $FF) | ((gt9_buf[base + 6] & $FF) << 8)
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    gt9_count = n
  EndIf

  ; ALWAYS clear on a frame that was seen, including the zero-touch frame.
  Gt9WriteReg(#GT9_REG_COOR, 0)
  ProcedureReturn gt9_count
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Gt9ScreenOff() - ask the controller to stop scanning. It wakes on the
;  next successful register read. Returns 1 or 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9ScreenOff()
  ProcedureReturn Gt9WriteReg(#GT9_REG_COMMAND, #GT9_CMD_SCREEN_OFF)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Accessors. An out-of-range index returns -1 rather than whatever is in
;  the array, so a caller that ignores the count gets an obviously wrong
;  number instead of a stale coordinate from the previous frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Gt9Addr()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_addr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9Count()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_count
EndProcedure

; Gt9ProdId() - the address of the four id bytes plus a terminating zero.
; "9271" or "911" on the parts this was written for. 0 if nothing
; answered.
Procedure.i Gt9ProdId()
  If gt9_addr = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn @gt9_idstr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9Firmware()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_fw
EndProcedure

; Gt9ConfigVersion() - the config block's version byte, or -1 if it would
; not read. -1 is not a failure of the driver; the controller is present.
Procedure.i Gt9ConfigVersion()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_cfg
EndProcedure

; Gt9XMax() / Gt9YMax() - the controller's OWN coordinate range, out of
; its config block. ZERO MEANS IT WOULD NOT SAY - either the block did
; not read or the block is unprogrammed - and zero is returned rather
; than a plausible default because the board scales every touch by these
; two numbers, and a made-up one puts every finger in the wrong place
; with nothing anywhere reporting an error.
Procedure.i Gt9XMax()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_xmax
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9YMax()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_ymax
EndProcedure

; Gt9MaxPoints() - contact slots the config block declares, capped at the
; part's ten. This is the number a frame's touch count is refused
; against.
Procedure.i Gt9MaxPoints()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_maxpts
EndProcedure

; Gt9IntTrigger() - the trigger type the config block asks for, 0..3, or
; -1 if the block would not read. RECORDED, NOT ACTED ON: there is no
; interrupt line to this controller on this board, so this driver polls
; whatever the number says. It is printed because a controller
; configured for a trigger nobody wired is worth seeing on a bring-up.
Procedure.i Gt9IntTrigger()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_trigger
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9LastBusCode()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_buscode
EndProcedure

; Gt9CodeHigh() / Gt9CodeLow() - what each address said the last time
; Gt9Begin asked it. #I2C_OK means it was never asked, or it answered.
Procedure.i Gt9CodeHigh()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_codehigh
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9CodeLow()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_codelow
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9Frames()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_frames
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9BadFrames()
  ProcedureReturn gt9_badframes
EndProcedure

; Gt9RawX / Gt9RawY - THE CONTROLLER'S OWN UNITS, 0 .. Gt9XMax()-1 and
; 0 .. Gt9YMax()-1. Not screen pixels: see the second block of the
; header for why the screen transform is not in this file.
Procedure.i Gt9RawX(i.i)
  If i < 0 Or i >= gt9_count
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gt9_x[i]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Gt9RawY(i.i)
  If i < 0 Or i >= gt9_count
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gt9_y[i]
EndProcedure

; Gt9Size(i) - how big the contact patch is; roughly, how hard.
Procedure.i Gt9Size(i.i)
  If i < 0 Or i >= gt9_count
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gt9_sz[i]
EndProcedure

; Gt9Id(i) - the tracking id. The controller keeps the same id on one
; finger while it slides, which is how a drag is followed and how a
; DOWN, a MOVE and an UP are told apart.
Procedure.i Gt9Id(i.i)
  If i < 0 Or i >= gt9_count
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gt9_id[i]
EndProcedure
